## Signing in

Access is through the **OpenSearch UI application**, not the domain's own
Dashboards address (that has no interactive sign-in).

1. Open the OpenSearch UI application URL (published by the platform team).
2. You are redirected to IAM Identity Center; sign in with your MoJ identity if
   you are not already signed in.
3. You land on the OpenSearch UI home page. Work happens inside a
   **workspace**. Open an existing one, or create one with the
   **Observability** use case and add the OpenSearch domain as its data source.

Your access follows your **Identity Center group membership** — being in the
right group is enough, there is nothing to request per person. Your group does
need to be added to the application by the platform team first; once it is,
everyone in that group can sign in.

## Access model

| Role | Who | What you can do |
|---|---|---|
| Read-only | Every business unit, by default | Search your unit's logs; build and read visualisations and dashboards. |
| Elevated | Units that opt in | Read-only, plus safe self-service such as exporting search results. |
| Admin | Platform team | Full management of the cluster. |

Two things follow:

- You see **only your own unit's logs** — your role is scoped to your unit's
  `<business-unit>-<environment>-*` indexes.
- The elevated role is **opt-in per unit**, and each unit maps its own teams to
  it, so onboarding an engineer needs no platform-team change.

The platform team defines and provisions the roles and manages the cluster;
business units map their own teams and own index-level access within their own
indexes.

## Finding your logs

Inside your workspace:

1. Open **Explore logs** (or **Discover**, the compass icon in the left-hand bar).
2. In the data selector choose **Indexes**, then the OpenSearch domain.
3. Type your unit's pattern, `<business-unit>-<environment>-*`, and choose
   **Add wildcard**. Don't pick a single index from the list: indexes are
   created daily, so a single one only covers one day.
4. Choose **Next**, set the time field to **`@timestamp`**, then **Select data**.
5. Set the time range (top right), for example **Last 1 hour**.

You can then search, add fields as columns, and build visualisations and
dashboards from the same data.

> In the development proof of concept, test indexes are named
> `fluentbit-<cluster>-<date>` rather than by business unit, so use
> `fluentbit-*` there.

## Programmatic access

For scripts and tools, call the domain's REST API directly with an AWS-signed
(SigV4) request using your `platform-engineer` credentials, rather than the
browser UI. Browser sign-in is for interactive use only.

## Troubleshooting

- **"You don't have authorization to access dashboards"** — your Identity Center
  group has not been added to the application yet. Contact the platform team with
  your group name so they can add it.
- **Signed in but no indexes** — logs may not be flowing for your unit yet, or
  your index pattern does not match `<business-unit>-<environment>-*`.
- **Every request fails with "Request failed to get to the server (status code:
  400)"**, even `GET /` in Dev Tools — this is a platform problem, not your
  access. The OpenSearch UI cannot pass your identity to the domain. Contact the
  platform team (the cause is described in ADR-017, OpenSearch section).
- **Only one day of logs shows** — you picked a single daily index. Go back to
  the data selector and add the wildcard pattern instead.
- **No results** — widen the time range, and check the time field is
  `@timestamp`.
- **Cannot reach the URL** — use the OpenSearch UI application URL, not the
  cluster's own Dashboards address.

